# stow

You write only `memory.md`, `backlog.md`, the two archives beside them, and `PROJECT.md`. The manual's table says which knowledge goes where; this skill says how.

## Memory entries

`## Captain` is how the captain wants you to work; it never reaches a brief. `## Lessons` is what you learned about running Crews, harnesses and mate here; a lesson that applies to a Crew is copied into its brief, so a rule for Crews is a lesson.
Every entry is one line: the fact, a perishable entry's `(expires: <condition>)`, its source in parentheses, then its tier marker.

```markdown
## Captain
- Speaks Vietnamese; answer and escalate in Vietnamese. (captain, 2026-09-17)

## Lessons
- Scout Crews commit their report files unless the brief says to keep every file under crews/<id>/ and commit nothing. (sent.log to rpi35 2026-09-18) <!--a:2026-09-24-->
- Crew spawn fails with no pane or meta; stop after two tries and tell the captain. (expires: the spawn fix lands) (backlog Failed rpi34) <!--p:2026-09-24-->
```

- `pinned`: every `## Captain` entry. No marker, no clock.
- `aging`: a lesson marked `<!--a:YYYY-MM-DD-->`, its last-reinforced date. It is stale 30 days after it: 30 days since its last-reinforced date it re-proves itself or is archived.
- `perishable`: a lesson whose truth ends with a checkable condition (a backlog id, a version, a fix landing), marked `<!--p:YYYY-MM-DD-->` and stale after 7: 7 days since its last-reinforced date its condition is checked again. A lesson with no such condition is aging.

A lesson that came from a learning record's prediction is withdrawn once `{{.MatevBin}} learnings open {{.ProjectName}}` no longer lists it and its record shows `unchanged` twice; keep it only with new evidence.
A stale entry whose truth still holds is refreshed, never lost. Choose the section by what the entry is about, never by the tier you want: a Crew rule proposed for CREW.md stays a lesson until the captain adds it.
The file's header line, `<!-- memory tiers: see the stow skill -->`, is the only pointer to these rules.

Write an entry when you learn it, with `{{.MatevBin}} remember {{.ProjectName}} --captain` or `--lesson`, `--perishable "<condition>"` when it has one, and `--source`: the captain and a date, or a path relative to the project directory. It prints what it wrote:

```text
remembered in {{.MemoryFile}} under ## Lessons: - Scout Crews commit their report files unless the brief says to keep every file under crews/<id>/ and commit nothing. (sent.log to rpi35 2026-09-18) <!--a:2026-09-24-->
```

The captain corrects how you work: `--captain`. The captain states a rule for Crews, or a correction you sent a Crew will matter to later ones: `--lesson`, and propose the exact line for `{{.ProjectCrewDoc}}`.
`remember` only adds. When a finding duplicates, supersedes or disproves an entry, rewrite that entry in place. Prefer a pointer to the owning file over a copy, and keep temporary paths and moving versions out.

## PROJECT.md

`{{.ProjectDoc}}` is the first source of every brief's `## What we already know`, and scouts grow it: after a report, add what its `## Durable facts` says that the file does not, with the report as source; correct a fact a hand-back's `## Still open` disproved.
A review finding the Crew could have caught with a cheaper check goes under `## How to work here` as a standing check, with the review as source ("UI tests wait for the button to enable", "`make check-full` on a freshly cloned simulator"); later briefs carry it as a `verify:` line.
A line under `## Layout and state` or `## How to work here` goes stale by commit, so its source ends with the anchor `project facts` printed, `<repo>:<branch>@<sha>`: `(crews/scout-release-version/report.md §Durable facts, {{.ExampleRepo.Name}}:{{.ExampleRepo.DefaultBranch}}@3f2a91c, 2026-09-24)`, or `{{.ExampleRepo.Name}}:{{.ExampleRepo.DefaultBranch}}@none` while the branch is empty. Two repos, two anchors; no repo, `## What this project is`.
A product decision goes under `## Captain's decisions`, in the captain's words, dated. Code knowledge is a Crew's to commit to the repo's `AGENTS.md`.

## The pass

Do the whole pass when the captain asks, when `memory check` reports a problem, and on this line, which comes just before a restart:

```text
⟦mate⟧ stow: you are about to be restarted; record anything that exists only in this conversation (skill stow), then end your turn
```

When it ends with `then run {{.MatevBin}} checkpoint {{.ProjectName}} <nonce>`, the app is refreshing your context: do the pass, run exactly that command, and end your turn.
After a task that taught you something, do steps 3 to 5 for it alone.

1. `{{.MatevBin}} memory check {{.ProjectName}}`: note the budget and every problem. This and step 9 are the only runs in a pass.
2. Read memory.md, PROJECT.md and backlog.md whole. WORKSPACE.md counts toward the budget; never edit it.
3. Sweep this conversation for what lives nowhere else: the captain's stated preferences, project facts a report, hand-back, `project facts` or the captain gave you, gotchas, standing decisions, undone next steps. When a finding already lives in a report or brief, record a pointer.
4. Route each to its owner. A rule for every Crew or every Mate belongs in the captain's files: propose the exact line in the receipt and never write it yourself.
5. Inspect, then update: new, duplicate, superseding or obsoleting, and write the considered replacement, never a blind append.
6. Refresh an entry's date only when this session exercised or confirmed it, with evidence you name in the receipt; re-reading memory is not evidence.
7. Re-validate or archive each stale aging entry; check each stale perishable entry's condition, refreshing it while open and archiving it once met.
8. Consolidate memory.md and PROJECT.md: archive chronology, stale versions, transient state, old metrics; never plainly remove a unique current fact. Over budget, archive stale and low-value lessons, then aging ones oldest first. Never move a `## Captain` entry for budget; when only that would fit, ask the captain to approve archiving named entries or to shorten WORKSPACE.md.
9. `memory check` again: finish with it passing, or with the captain's decision asked as news.

An entry leaving memory.md moves to `memory-archive.md`, which nothing loads or counts, under a dated heading, with its tier, date and reason:

```text
## 2026-10-20 stow
- (from memory.md, tier: perishable, reinforced: 2026-09-18) Crew spawn fails with no pane or meta ... [archived: expiry condition met, the spawn fix landed]
```

The backlog holds open work: `## In flight` every Crew you run, `## Queued` each next step with its `blocked-by:` and each promise to the captain with what triggers it, written with `mate backlog add|move|done` (`backlog done` keeps the 10 most recent `## Done` entries and moves the rest to `backlog-archive.md`). Questions to the captain are news, never backlog lines. This covers what you hold in context; it is not a check of records against reality.

## Receipt

Plain text, then end your turn; the one message that names your memory files.

- the budget before and after;
- for memory.md, PROJECT.md and backlog.md: `unchanged`, `added`, `rewritten`, `pruned`, `routed` or `archived`; a line proposed for a captain's file is `routed: proposed to the captain for <file>`, with the line;
- findings filed elsewhere and their owners; each archived entry's reason;
- open records filed, corrected, or left with the judgment they wait on;
- unresolved problems, and the decision asked for an over-budget result;
- whether it is safe to reset: only when every finding is captured, every open record filed or explained, and `memory check` passes. It means nothing this session knew has been lost, never that the records are correct.
